WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.701
-Our first guest tonight
is a Golden Globe-winning actor

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:04.137 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know from such films
as "Moulin Rouge!"

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:04.938 line:92%
and the "Star Wars" prequels

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:07.741
as well as his Emmy-nominated
work in the FX series "Fargo."

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:11.244
He stars in "Christopher Robin",
which is in theaters tomorrow.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.379 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.247 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-This place is very big.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:16.182 line:92%
Do you live here all alone?

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Just now, yes,
but usually, no.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.887 size:75% position:38% line:92%
My wife and daughter

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:23.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
are in the countryside
for the weekend.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:26.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, why -- [ grunts ] --
aren't you with them?

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:29.095 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well --
[ Sighs ]

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.530 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Sticky business.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.631 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-I had to say for work.

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:34.801
Why are you here and not
in the forest with your friends?

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.202 size:75% position:38% line:92%
That's the question.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:39.039 line:92%
-Well, I couldn't find anybody.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.341 line:92%
I couldn't find anyone, either.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.878 line:92%
And I looked for them both --

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:46.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hello?
-[ Sighs ]

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
Piglet?
Eeyore?

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:50.817 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Tigger?

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:51.951 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Where is everybody?

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:55.088
-Exactly.
Where is Tigger or Kanga or Roo?

00:00:55.088 --> 00:00:56.556 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-That's why I'm here.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:59.859
-Please welcome back to the show
Ewan McGregor, everyone!

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.195 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:08.068 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:13.540 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
♪♪♪♪

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.774 line:92%
-So lovely to have you back.

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:18.078 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm glad to be here.
Very nice.

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:19.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This is very exciting,
you are --

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
For those who couldn't figure
it out from the clip...

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:21.981 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
-...you're an adult
Christopher Robin.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
A grown-up Christopher Robin.

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:26.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's very exciting.
-Yeah, it's nice.

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:29.189
It's nice to take someone that
we know iconically like that,

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
like, a young --
I've done it the other way.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.359
In the "Star Wars" films,
I took an older character,

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:36.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was iconic, and tried
to play him as a younger man.

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:37.931 line:92%
And this time, it's the reverse.

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:38.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, that's --
-Yeah.

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:40.667
-You'll have to let me how
this one works out for you.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:41.734 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, we'll see.
We'll see how it goes.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:45.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And, obviously, an incredible
amount of CGI in this film.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:48.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
Well, yes.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.810 size:88% position:44%
It's so funny, because
I remember it like that.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.314
Like, I remember,
we had a little Winnie the Pooh

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:55.148 line:92%
who was like a Teddy bear.

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:57.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
He wasn't a puppet or
he wasn't animated in any way.

00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:59.419
-'Cause you couldn't get
the real Winnie the Pooh, right?

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.488 align:start size:94% position:6%
He turned down the project.
-He was too -- Yeah.

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:02.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-He was too expensive.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, of course.
-That's the problem.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we had
this little Teddy bear,

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:07.994 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then, a Teddy bear
for each of the creatures.

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:10.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Marc Forster,
our brilliant director,

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:14.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
cast this group
of enthusiastic young actors,

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:16.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
most of them
just out of drama school,

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:18.171 line:92%
to play each of the creatures.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.073 line:92%
So when we were playing a scene,

00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:22.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, they would be there
with the Teddy bear.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:25.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
They'd move him around
and do the voice

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:28.047 line:92%
and act with us in the scenes.

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:28.781 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And then improvise.

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:31.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
We wanted to go away
from the text and improvise.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:34.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
They would improvise with us,
and they were brilliant.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I got used
to his little face,

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:36.890 align:start size:94% position:6%
because he looked
just like that.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, it was like acting
with a little bear.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:40.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:42.795
-So when I watched the film
for the first time, I sort of --

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:46.366
I kept having to remind myself
that he wasn't there on the day.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:47.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Right, yeah.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:49.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
-'Cause it's just sort of
how I remember it.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:51.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
And because we know
the characters so well,

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:53.673 line:92%
I could imagine how he might be.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:54.674 line:92%
But they did him so beautifully.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:57.010 size:88% position:44%
-It's really wonderful.
And the voice is someone

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.612
who voiced him in the 1980
"Winnie the Pooh" cartoons.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:02.815
-Jim Cummings.
-Yeah, which is incredibly nice

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:04.250 line:92%
to hear such a familiar voice.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:04.817 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
It would be weird

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
if Winnie the Pooh
sounded different.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Someone else, yes.
-If he had, like,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
a thick New York accent,
it would be a huge --

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:10.423 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.292 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Or Scottish.
-Yeah, Scottish, yeah.

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:13.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Scottish accent ]
Christopher!

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.627 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Christopher Robin!

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Scottish accent ]
Get your head out of your ass!

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:18.531 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:19.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Normal voice ]
That was very good.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:21.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Normal voice ]
Oh, thank you very much.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.936
I can't believe I had the nerve
to do my B-minus

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:24.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
Scottish impression
in front of you.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.371 line:92%
-That was really good, yeah.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:27.607
-But now, you would think
a child would be excited

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:31.945
to come on set and see these
actors holding these puppets.

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:33.112 size:84% position:42% line:92%
But some of the puppets

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:36.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
would maybe freak out a child
if they came.

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:37.283 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yes. We had --

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:41.921
The truth is, we had the
beautiful finished product,

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.289 line:92%
the lovely bear to act with.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:44.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was for
the visual effects people

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:46.826
to see what he looked like
in the light and everything.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:52.298
But they couldn't animate him
on top of that hairy bear,

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:54.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
so we had --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:56.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just said "hairy bear"
on television.

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:57.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-But we had --

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:57.904 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:59.372
-A lot of people who were, like,
in the kitchen

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:01.541
and had totally tuned this out,
were like, "Oh!"

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:04.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"Wait a minute."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.347 size:78% position:39% line:92%
So they had a gray --

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:09.616 line:92%
Just a canvas bear with no hair.

00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:10.617 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And they, I guess,

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:12.986
they would animate him
on top of that gray bear.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:16.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
But they also sometimes
needed one without a head.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you'd do takes
with the gray headless bear.

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:20.193 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:21.861
And then,
there was a real horror show one

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:26.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
where it was just his torso --
no arms, legs, or head.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:29.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was like the
Guillermo del Toro version.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:30.436 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:33.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then they had an arm
on a stick, a Pooh --

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
A Pooh on a stick.
They had an arm...

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:35.808 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:36.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
...on a stick.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they had his little feet
on sticks, as well,

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:42.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
for the little footstep shots
and stuff so...

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
-See, that's all haunting.
-Yeah.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:46.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Although, they could
use the torso and do a sequel

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:49.722
where Tigger comes and says,
"Something awful's happened."

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:50.323 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:52.492 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"I lost my mind.
I don't know what I --"

00:04:52.492 --> 00:04:53.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"I don't know."
-"I don't know what I did."

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:55.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"I did something.
I woke up, I did something!"

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:56.629 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:58.564 align:start size:94% position:6%
Have you read the books?
Is that something --

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:01.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. I mean,
I read them to all my girls.

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:01.868 size:81% position:41% line:92%
The first one, anyway.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a series
of four or five books.

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:05.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I'm familiar
with the first volume.

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.907
And then I'm sure I must have
been read it when I was a kid,

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:10.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause I'm so familiar
with those stories.

00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:10.810 line:92%
-And then this sort of --

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.679 size:78% position:39%
this film takes place
post-World War II.

00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:15.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
And did the timing
of when this was happening

00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:17.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
inform how you wanted to play
Christopher Robin?

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:20.119 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
I love that era.

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:21.187 line:92%
I watched quite a lot film --

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:23.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
I watched a lot of
early Alec Guinness films.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a great film called
"Man in a White Suit"

00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:26.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I watched.
-Mm-hmm.

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:28.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And there's a British film
I watched,

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:28.895 size:88% position:44% line:92%
"From Here to Eternity."

00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:30.463
[ Proper British accent ]
And anything where everyone

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:31.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
spoke like that.
You know.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-"Are you happy, darling?"

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Terribly."
That kind of thing.

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:37.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Normal voice ]
And there was one film

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.104 line:92%
that was very handy to me.

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:39.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a film called
"Genevieve."

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:40.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay.
-Which was about

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:44.477
a vintage car race
in the early '50s or late '40s.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:47.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that one was --
the main character in that

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.047 line:92%
is quite like Christopher Robin.

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:50.650
-That's nice that you got to --
that's a very wonderful thing

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:51.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be able to do research
for your work

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:53.720 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is just, like,
watching cool old movies.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:54.620 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Watching old films.
-Yeah.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
-'Cause I grew up
watching those old films.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so there's --
I feel like I've been waiting

00:05:58.825 --> 00:06:01.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
to play this kind of guy
for a long time.

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
-When you were young
and watching films like that,

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:05.598
were other people your age
watching films like that?

00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:08.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because that seems a little...
[ Light laughter ]

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:09.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I don't think --
Well, I don't know.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:12.238 align:start size:94% position:6%
On the weekends, on BBC Two,
they'd have like matinees

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:15.842
on a Saturday and Sunday, they'd
play two old films back to back.

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:18.811
And I would always be propped up
on my elbows watching them.

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:19.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I don't know.
I didn't --

00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:21.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I do think that I,
looking back,

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:24.784
I saw a lot of old films because
there was nothing else on.

00:06:24.784 --> 00:06:26.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And Netflix didn't exist.

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:27.787 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's right.
-Yeah.

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:30.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So you'd be like,
"You tell me, TV."

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:30.556 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, "What is it?"
-"Yeah, it's up to you."

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:32.792 line:92%
-"What's it gonna be today?"

00:06:32.792 --> 00:06:34.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, exactly.
No, that's right.

